{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1960 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "b929598d021693f822f8aa4bafef8af7e5eb59528b3b8cdbd691842473b59133" :edb/db-before-t 1959 :edb/db-after-t 1960 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535272 50 #inst "2026-10-08T20:59:04.773Z" 13194139535272 true] [17592186092111 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341075 13194139535272 true] [17592186092112 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341078 13194139535272 true] [17592186092112 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341084 13194139535272 true] [17592186092118 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341068 13194139535272 true] [17592186092118 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341089 13194139535272 true] [17592186341068 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341069 13194139535272 true] [17592186341068 1089 "q-120896" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341068 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535272 true] [17592186341068 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{7}{9}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ to the nearest integer?" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341068 1113 #uuid "2c54e7d4-028f-5b21-afe7-a0c2c94b02e4" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341068 1145 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{7}{9}) & =2.461\\,918…≈2.461\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.461\\,9$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=2.461\\,9.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -2.461\\,9 & ≈0.679\\,7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.679\\,7.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/assets/eafc9be7094c9c0ccdf167cc737d494efec40014a89ad793f7ed34ea5eed94ae.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.679\\,7 \\\\  & ≈3.821\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2.461\\,9\\cdot 3.821\\,3≈9\n$$\n\nto the nearest integer." 13194139535272 true] [17592186341069 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341070 13194139535272 true] [17592186341069 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341071 13194139535272 true] [17592186341069 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341072 13194139535272 true] [17592186341069 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341073 13194139535272 true] [17592186341069 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341074 13194139535272 true] [17592186341069 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535272 true] [17592186341069 1041 "selection" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341069 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341070 13194139535272 true] [17592186341069 1110 #uuid "d619dae3-71a5-54ca-96fa-4620bde81b8c" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341070 1034 "9" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341070 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535272 true] [17592186341070 1111 #uuid "3f6585d2-8d47-5fd8-8ead-623f2f938ecf" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341071 1034 "10" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341071 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535272 true] [17592186341071 1111 #uuid "a77bb6d5-3403-5cb2-be7a-22f9e89ebbcf" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341072 1034 "8" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341072 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535272 true] [17592186341072 1111 #uuid "38c504b4-1e59-5adb-bd98-489664ad1aba" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341073 1034 "7" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341073 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535272 true] [17592186341073 1111 #uuid "a4ff9b81-2481-5747-bc08-c47ead841607" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341074 1034 "6" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341074 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535272 true] [17592186341074 1111 #uuid "44ea278d-01ad-5af8-9cc5-90e52de2e2c2" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341075 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341076 13194139535272 true] [17592186341075 1089 "q-274589" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341075 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535272 true] [17592186341075 1100 "The solution to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-1$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139535272 true] [17592186341075 1113 #uuid "f1671010-6b83-59b3-97e5-15e231262d67" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341075 1145 "Notice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its minimum value.\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1)=\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\pi $ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x=\\pi .$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its minimum values at\n\n$$\nx=\\pi +2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSince the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x=\\pi $ is the only solution." 13194139535272 true] [17592186341076 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341077 13194139535272 true] [17592186341076 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535272 true] [17592186341076 1041 "field-1" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341076 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341077 13194139535272 true] [17592186341076 1110 #uuid "6589e7e6-be89-5fea-8d47-fdf742ef6b5a" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341077 1034 "\\pi" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341077 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535272 true] [17592186341077 1111 #uuid "f78f5cee-28b4-5244-91fa-cdb434b2f2c0" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341078 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341079 13194139535272 true] [17592186341078 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341082 13194139535272 true] [17592186341078 1089 "q-338574" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341078 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535272 true] [17592186341078 1100 "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535272 true] [17592186341078 1113 #uuid "50552061-b7c2-53bb-b8b9-b421209c9dbd" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341078 1145 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/assets/a22dba264faa711352724f135d2ec5a074202c4b5069dee2389fe954419a3770.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}-2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341079 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341080 13194139535272 true] [17592186341079 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341081 13194139535272 true] [17592186341079 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535272 true] [17592186341079 1041 "field-1" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341079 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341081 13194139535272 true] [17592186341079 1110 #uuid "5a440afb-8163-5750-b01f-bfaee87ad826" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341080 1034 "0" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341080 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535272 true] [17592186341080 1111 #uuid "a24b3945-a887-57b0-ab23-12bb6c3cdfb5" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341081 1034 "-\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341081 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535272 true] [17592186341081 1111 #uuid "e1a8e648-cb33-5110-b2ae-ae9dcab518e4" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341082 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341083 13194139535272 true] [17592186341082 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535272 true] [17592186341082 1041 "field-2" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341082 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341083 13194139535272 true] [17592186341082 1110 #uuid "ff8b6a73-8e21-5574-bcb8-694a83de4e7f" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341083 1034 "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341083 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535272 true] [17592186341083 1111 #uuid "a81ae004-1067-5ca5-ae95-f9f2bf612e27" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341084 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341085 13194139535272 true] [17592186341084 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341087 13194139535272 true] [17592186341084 1089 "q-338710" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341084 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535272 true] [17592186341084 1100 "Arranged in **ascending order** and rounded to **the nearest integer**, the solutions of the equation $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535272 true] [17592186341084 1113 #uuid "c06764c8-676c-57ec-8676-1a7cc0d09408" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341084 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2 & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{5}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{5}{8})=2.245\\,927…≈2.246\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.246$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution, rounded to the nearest integer, is $x=2.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.896.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.896.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/assets/992325c7cbf34f4b981af0a9f34740990d39694e727861ca92bf591037436327.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.896 \\\\  & ≈4.038\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =4.038-2\\pi  \\\\  & ≈-2.245 \\\\  & ≈-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341085 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341086 13194139535272 true] [17592186341085 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535272 true] [17592186341085 1041 "field-1" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341085 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341086 13194139535272 true] [17592186341085 1110 #uuid "3d1fa6f5-0a0e-59b4-b064-8f5a81099c18" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341086 1034 "-2" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341086 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535272 true] [17592186341086 1111 #uuid "f0492d6d-030f-5961-b2fc-3eda940bf6da" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341087 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341088 13194139535272 true] [17592186341087 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535272 true] [17592186341087 1041 "field-2" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341087 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341088 13194139535272 true] [17592186341087 1110 #uuid "5a03e003-4760-5ad0-8561-c30ee6522015" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341088 1034 "2" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341088 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535272 true] [17592186341088 1111 #uuid "26787f46-7591-5023-b5f9-5cb20f0b5e30" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341089 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341090 13194139535272 true] [17592186341089 1089 "q-72835" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341089 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535272 true] [17592186341089 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ to the nearest integer?" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341089 1113 #uuid "8530205e-bf53-5ec7-b72c-631c4c27fbb1" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341089 1145 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{2}{3}) & =2.300\\,523…≈2.301\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.301$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=2.301.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -2.301 & ≈0.841.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.841.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061537/assets/d02a0250df0f0e6c389594ea0e1b119d9f4c63c353553d56c0e865f487862284.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.841 \\\\  & ≈3.983\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}≈2.301\\cdot 3.983≈9\n$$\n\nto the nearest integer." 13194139535272 true] [17592186341090 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341091 13194139535272 true] [17592186341090 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341092 13194139535272 true] [17592186341090 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341093 13194139535272 true] [17592186341090 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341094 13194139535272 true] [17592186341090 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341095 13194139535272 true] [17592186341090 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535272 true] [17592186341090 1041 "selection" 13194139535272 true] [17592186341090 1104 #edb/re